Technology choices and deep reasoning
Why Node.js + ws (raw WebSocket library)?
WebSockets give you a persistent, bidirectional connection — unlike HTTP which is request-response. Once connected, the server can push to clients without them asking. That's essential for real-time state sync.
We use Node.js because its event loop model handles thousands of concurrent connections with a single thread — each connection is just an event listener, not an OS thread. A Java thread-per-connection model would exhaust memory at ~10,000 connections. Node's model scales to 100,000+.
We use ws (not Socket.IO) because Socket.IO adds abstraction layers (rooms, namespaces, fallback transports) that obscure what's actually happening. For learning the hard engineering, raw ws forces you to understand the problems directly.
Why a game loop with fixed tick rate?
Instead of processing every message as it arrives (reactive), the server runs a fixed-interval loop (e.g., every 50ms = 20 ticks/second). All incoming messages queue up, and each tick processes the queue in order. This solves the "simultaneous action" problem — within a tick, actions get processed sequentially with a deterministic order rule (e.g., by arrival timestamp, then by player ID as tiebreaker).
This is the same pattern used in every serious real-time game (Valve's Source Engine, Unity Netcode, etc.).
Why sequence numbers on every message?
Every server message carries a sequence number. Clients track the last sequence they received. If they see seq=47 after seq=45, they know seq=46 was dropped — they can request a resync. Without sequence numbers, you can't detect gaps.
Why Redis for room state?
If you store game state in-process (plain JS object), one server can run one set of rooms. When you need to scale to multiple servers, rooms need to live somewhere all servers can read/write. Redis is an in-memory key-value store with pub/sub — perfect for this. (In this project we simulate it with a clean in-process store you can swap for Redis with one function change.)
Why not a database like PostgreSQL for live game state?
PostgreSQL is disk-backed. Writing game state 20 times per second per room to disk is catastrophically slow. You use PostgreSQL for persistent data (player accounts, match history, final scores). Live game state lives in Redis-style in-memory stores.
Why Lambert's logical clocks / vector clocks instead of wall-clock timestamps for ordering?
Wall clocks on different machines drift. Player A's laptop clock might be 340ms ahead of Player B's. If you order by timestamp, Player B's action always appears to happen "earlier" even if it arrived first. Logical clocks (Lamport) are monotonically increasing numbers the server controls — independent of any machine's clock.
